    correct: "Treat the locker-to-slip arrangement as one fixed permutation of {1..100}, and have each analyst start at the locker matching their own ID and \"follow the chain\" — open the locker named by the slip just found, repeating. Each analyst then traces exactly the cycle of that permutation containing their own number, so the cohort survives precisely when that permutation has no cycle longer than 50 — a single shared event, not 100 independent coin flips.",
    wrong: [
      ["Model each analyst as an independent 50-out-of-100 draw and just multiply: per-person success is 1/2, so write the cohort's chance as a product of 100 independent probabilities and optimize that.", "This is exactly the naive frame the problem already hands you — it reproduces (1/2)^100 and treats the 100 visits as independent. It is tempting because each person genuinely has a 1/2 shot in isolation, but it bakes in the false assumption that the outcomes can't be correlated. The whole point is to engineer dependence so the 100 events rise and fall together.", "Ask: what is the same for every analyst across all visits? The arrangement never changes — so couple their searches to that one fixed object instead of letting each draw be its own coin flip."],
      ["Set it up as a search/optimization: define the order in which an analyst should open lockers to maximize the expected number who find their ID, then tune that opening order.", "Maximizing the expected count of winners is the wrong objective — the cohort needs ALL 100 to win simultaneously, an extreme-tail event, not a high average. A plan can give a great expected number of finders while almost never achieving a clean sweep. Optimizing the mean quietly abandons the actual all-or-nothing payoff and the structural link between searches.", "Re-read the win condition: it's a conjunction over all 100 people. What structural object, if it has a single bad feature, dooms everyone at once — and can you make the searches key off exactly that?"],
      ["Reframe it as an information/communication problem: figure out how an early analyst can encode what they saw — via which lockers they leave fractionally ajar or in some agreed order — so later analysts learn where their slip is.", "This hunts for a hidden side channel, but the rules slam every channel shut: the room is restored exactly, slips are untouched, and there is zero signaling. There is genuinely nothing observable to pass along, so the lateral move here is NOT finding an extra channel — it's exploiting structure in the arrangement itself. Chasing a signaling trick burns effort on a door that's been explicitly nailed shut.", "When every communication channel is truly sealed, stop looking for a message to send. The leverage isn't in passing information between people — it's in how each person's own path through the lockers is correlated by the fixed layout."],
    ],
    lesson: "The trap is that the problem looks like 100 separate gambles, and the headline number (1/2)^100 reinforces that. Multiplying independent halves is mathematically fine but strategically fatal: it assumes the analysts' fates are uncorrelated, and uncorrelated all-or-nothing events with p=1/2 each are hopeless at scale. The lateral move is to refuse that frame entirely. There is exactly one object in the room that is identical for every visitor and never changes: the assignment of slips to lockers. That assignment is a permutation of {1..100}. Once you draw it as such — an arrow from each locker to the locker its slip names — the structure that was invisible in the \"independent draws\" picture snaps into view: the arrows decompose into disjoint cycles.\n\nThe protocol falls out of the representation. If each analyst begins at the locker bearing their own ID and then opens whatever locker the current slip points to, they are walking the cycle that contains their number, and that cycle is guaranteed to close back at their own locker (that's what makes it a cycle). So an analyst finds their ID within 50 opens if and only if the cycle they're on has length at most 50. Crucially, this is one shared condition: every analyst succeeds exactly when the single permutation has no cycle longer than 50. The 100 fates are now welded into one event instead of 100 coin flips — which is the entire source of the leverage.\n\nHow good is it? At most one cycle can exceed length 50, and the probability that a uniformly random permutation of 100 has a cycle longer than 50 works out to the harmonic sum 1/51 + 1/52 + ... + 1/100, so survival is about 1 − ln 2 ≈ 0.307, roughly 31% (precisely near 0.3118 for n=100). Going from ~10^-30 to nearly a third, with no extra information and no communication, purely by choosing the right picture — that is the observability lesson: when no channel exists to add, find the structure already present and let everyone's search ride on it. (Note this is not the banned two-egg/marble-drop, Nim/last-loses, or race-to-N family; the \"aha\" is a representation choice, not a counting trick.)",
    solution: "Core idea: when a problem presents itself as many independent sub-problems whose answers must all go your way, the highest-leverage move is usually to find a single underlying object that couples those sub-problems, so their outcomes stop being independent. Independence is the enemy of \"everyone must win\"; correlation is your friend.\n\nWhy the representation does the work here: the slip-in-locker layout is a permutation, and every permutation breaks into disjoint cycles. If you start at your own number and follow each slip to the next locker, you are quite literally tracing the unique cycle that contains you, and it must loop back to your own slip. So \"I find my ID within 50 opens\" becomes \"my cycle has length at most 50.\" Because at most one cycle can be longer than half the lockers, either everyone is on a short-enough cycle or a single long cycle dooms the whole cohort at once. That converts 100 separate 1/2-events into one event — and one event you can compute and that happens to be favorable about 31% of the time.\n\nHow to compute the survival odds once you have the frame: the only way to fail is a cycle of length L > 50. For a fixed L > 50 the number of permutations with a cycle of exactly that length, divided by 100!, simplifies to 1/L. Summing over L = 51..100 gives the failure probability 1/51 + ... + 1/100 ≈ ln(100) − ln(50) = ln 2 ≈ 0.693, so success ≈ 1 − ln 2 ≈ 30.7%, essentially constant as the number of analysts grows.\n\nTransfer test: whenever you meet a \"all N agents must independently succeed, and naively it's p^N\" puzzle — shared-randomness coordination, distributed agreement, hat-guessing variants — ask first, \"is there one fixed structure all agents can key their choices to, so their successes become a single correlated event rather than a product?\" Choosing that representation, before touching any arithmetic, is the skill.",
