    lesson: "The trap is to count odds. Any one diver, staring at four hats, genuinely has a 1-in-5 shot, and it feels like the team is stuck at \"expected one correct, but maybe zero.\" The lateral move is to stop asking \"what can I deduce about my own hat?\" (answer: nothing — every value 0-4 is still possible for you) and instead ask \"what about this arrangement is already frozen and shared by everyone?\" That object is the grand total of all five numbers reduced mod 5. It is an invariant in the purest sense: no diver chose it, no diver can change it, and it sits in exactly the five-element set {0,1,2,3,4}. Crucially, each diver *almost* knows it — she sees the sum of the other four, so the total mod 5 is her visible sum plus her own unknown number. That single missing residue is the only thing she lacks, and it is the same missing quantity for everyone.\n\nNow the protocol writes itself. Assign the five divers the five residues 0,1,2,3,4, one each. Diver k assumes \"the true total mod 5 is my residue k,\" and since she knows the sum of the other four, she solves for the one hat value that would make that true and writes it down. The true total mod 5 is some definite value; it equals exactly one diver's assigned residue (the residues form a complete set, so precisely one matches). That diver's assumption is correct, so her deduced value is her actual value — a guaranteed hit. Everyone else may miss, but the team never all-misses. The successes are perfectly anti-correlated by construction, which is why the naive \"independent 1/5 each\" intuition undercounts to zero-possible.\n\nThe deeper lesson, and the interview-relevant one, is that you found an extra observable not by adding a channel but by noticing one the structure handed you for free: a globally shared, conserved residue. The art is choosing an invariant whose number of states equals the number of agents, so the agents can partition its possible values among themselves and blanket every case. A parity bit (two states) is too coarse for five divers; the sum mod 5 (five states) fits exactly. Same idea scales to n divers with hats in {0,...,n-1}: total mod n, n distinct residues, one guaranteed correct, always.",
    solution: "Goal: guarantee that, for ANY hat assignment, at least one of n players guessing simultaneously and silently is correct.\n\nSetup recap:\n- n players, each hat stamped with a number in {0, 1, ..., n-1}. Repeats allowed; assignment is adversarial.\n- Each player sees all OTHER hats, never her own, and all write one guess at the same instant with no signalling.\n\nStep 1 - Find the frozen, shared quantity. The sum S of all n numbers, reduced mod n, is determined the moment the hats are on. No player can see it or change it, and it lives in the n-element set {0,...,n-1}. This is the invariant.\n\nStep 2 - Note what each player almost knows. Player k sees the others, so she knows (S minus her own number) mod n. She is missing exactly one residue: her own number mod n. So the whole game reduces to \"the one unknown residue everyone shares is S mod n.\"\n\nStep 3 - Partition the invariant's values. Before hats go on, assign player k the residue a_k = k, so the assignments 0,1,...,n-1 are all distinct and cover every possible value of S mod n.\n\nStep 4 - Each player bets her assigned residue is the true total. Player k computes her guess g_k as the unique number in {0,...,n-1} satisfying:\n   (sum of visible hats) + g_k ≡ a_k (mod n).\nEquivalently g_k ≡ a_k − (S − x_k) (mod n), where x_k is her (unknown) true number.\n\nStep 5 - Why exactly one is right. Player k is correct precisely when g_k = x_k, which happens precisely when a_k ≡ S (mod n). Because the a_k run through all n residues exactly once, exactly one of them equals S mod n. That one player is guaranteed correct; the rest may be wrong. Hence \"at least one correct,\" always.\n\nWorked check (n = 3, hats = [2, 2, 1], so S = 5, S mod 3 = 2):\n- Player 0 (residue 0) sees 2+1=3; guesses g with 3+g ≡ 0 (mod 3) → g = 0. Wrong (true 2).\n- Player 1 (residue 1) sees 2+1=3; guesses g with 3+g ≡ 1 (mod 3) → g = 1. Wrong (true 2).\n- Player 2 (residue 2) sees 2+2=4; guesses g with 4+g ≡ 2 (mod 3) → g = 1. Correct (true 1). \nThe matching player is the one whose residue equals S mod 3 = 2, exactly as predicted.\n\nTakeaway: when several agents must act simultaneously and silently, look for a conserved, globally shared quantity with as many states as there are agents, then have each agent claim a different state. The agents can't communicate, but the invariant already \"speaks\" to all of them at once.",
